  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 203 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nCurrent H5 analysis shows a deep closed low (nor'easter) off \nthe coast of New Jersey, with mid/upper ridging across the\nwestern CONUS east of another trough making landfall across the\nWest Coast, with weak north northwesterly H5 flow in place \nacross our CWA. At the surface, light and variable winds are \npresent between a surface high over northern Wisconsin and a \nsubtle surface low near the Red River. A relatively saturated\nboundary layer has yielded persistent low level cloud cover with\nsome patchy drizzle and even a few light showers toward central\nMissouri. Isolated pop up showers will be possible later this\nafternoon, but most areas should remain dry. For tonight, mostly\ncloudy skies should persist, with perhaps some patchy fog and\ntemperatures dropping into the upper 50s to lower 60s by early\nMonday morning. \n\nTemperatures should warm a bit Monday and into Tuesday as the\nwestern trough digs into southern California and Hurricane Polo\napproaches Baja California, sending the western ridge axis\ncloser to our CWA. As we head into Wednesday and Thursday, the\nremnants of Polo's moisture plume is progged to get absorbed by\nthe deepening upper trough as it crosses the Southern Rockies\ninto the Southern Plains and enhanced south southwesterly flow \noverspreads our CWA. With PWATs on the order of 1.8 to 2 inches\nand ample forcing for ascent, scattered to widespread showers \nand thunderstorms are likely to begin by late Tuesday night \nacross far NW Missouri, expanding in coverage to cover the\nentire CWA by Wednesday and into Thursday. Some differences\nremain in where exactly models want to place the heaviest\nrainfall amounts, and most recent guidance has actually\ndecreased QPF across the CWA. However, current grids still show \nroughly 2 to 3 inches of rain across the region for Tuesday\nevening through Thursday evening, with highest amounts across NW\nMissouri and NE Kansas. Cloud cover and some patchy showers may\nlinger into Friday morning, but at least some sunshine and dry \nconditions should return by Friday evening and into the weekend,\nwith daily high temperatures ranging from the upper 60s to \nlower 70s and overnight lows in the 50s.",
